Subject: [ballyalley] Re: Video timing













            For my first question, what I actually want to know is how much

processing time we have while the system isn't displaying graphics

(while displaying the lower border, bringing the video beam back up

and drawing the upper border).



Regarding my second question, what I was thinking is that a program,

using some well placed horizontal lines (for instance, between the

score display and the playfield, at the ground, or everywhere there

is a single-colored horizontal line) the programmer could disable the

screen, set the border color to match the desired color, and create

a "fake" screen line, while the memory which was going to be used

(wasted) to generate it could be reused as normal memory, without

affecting the screen display. At the next line, border color is

corrected, the video brought back online and the image starts being

displayed in the regular way again, as if nothing happened.


One thing you CAN do is put some machine language on the lower part of the screen, or even use it as temporary ram. You can the change the video interupts to not display that area by setting the background color and the foreground color to the same number. HOWEVER if you CLEAR the screen or somehow let any on your graphics into that area, all information there will be LOST.
As far as trying to do things during the interrupt timing, remember that the Astrocade uses a  micro cycler and that it "shares" the video with the program "words"   (2 bytes). This makes utilizing the interrupt system during the video extremely complicated.  I not saying it can't be done, but you are really asking for a lot here from the processor.
Ken


And for the third question, I am just curious to know if the

interrupts are generated for border lines, too. In this case, each

border line could have a unique color (euch!).



Ronaldo Goulart



